# Response headers for header-capable static hosts (Cloudflare Pages, Netlify — both read a
# `_headers` file at the root of the PUBLISHED directory, which is what `docs/` is).
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS. The app's CSP lives in a <meta> tag because GitHub Pages cannot set response
# headers at all — and `frame-ancestors` and `X-Frame-Options` are header-ONLY directives, ignored
# entirely in a meta CSP. That is the gap CLAUDE.md describes as needing "a real host or proxy in
# front": until now the clickjacking defence has been `docs/js/framebust.js` plus a CSS
# `display: none`, which works but is a workaround for a header we simply could not send.
# On a host that reads this file, the header-based defence finally exists.
#
# IT DOES NOT REPLACE THE EXISTING DEFENCE, and must never be treated as doing so. `framebust.js`
# and the `html[data-framed]` CSS suppression stay exactly as they are: this file has no effect on
# GitHub Pages or `node server.js`, so removing the in-page defence would leave those two deployments
# completely unprotected. CLAUDE.md's rule stands — never make the CSS suppression conditional.
#
# The CSP below carries ONLY frame-ancestors. That is deliberate: multiple CSP policies are each
# enforced independently rather than merged, so a header policy listing one directive restricts framing
# and nothing else, leaving index.html's full meta CSP (which enumerates every RPC host in
# connect-src) as the single place script/connect policy is maintained. Duplicating the whole policy
# here would create a second list to keep in sync with PUBLIC_RPCS, and `tools/verify-ui.mjs` asserts
# that agreement against the meta tag — a drifting copy in this file would silently block RPC
# endpoints and look like an endpoint failure.

/*
  Content-Security-Policy: frame-ancestors 'none'
  X-Frame-Options: DENY
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  # No path or query from this app should ever leak to a third party via Referer. The app talks to
  # public RPC endpoints and DefiLlama with the wallet address in flight; there is no reason for any
  # of them to receive a referrer.
  Referrer-Policy: no-referrer
  # Nothing here uses a camera, microphone, geolocation or payment handler. Denying them outright
  # means a future dependency cannot quietly start asking.
  Permissions-Policy: accelerometer=(), camera=(), geolocation=(), gyroscope=(), magnetometer=(), microphone=(), payment=(), usb=()
  Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy: same-origin
